#: includes/server-rules-render.php:641
msgid "ISPConfig nginx Directives: robots.txt and reject before PHP"
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:642
msgid "ISPConfig: Sites, Website, Options, nginx Directives. Paste it once: a second ##merge## block makes nginx -t fail with \"try_files directive is duplicate\". ISPConfig removes lines starting with # and replaces a location it already has, so this text has no comments and no \"location /\". The first block adds try_files to ISPConfig's own location = /robots.txt, so robots.txt reaches WordPress. The rule hash is in the X-Jpkpf-Rule line."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:648
msgid "Reject before PHP (server block)"
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:649
msgid "Inside the site's server block, at server level (not inside a location), then nginx -t and a reload. No root needed if you may edit the site's server block. Rejected requests go to an internal location: they never reach PHP or the rate limit."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:678
msgid "Rate-limit zones (http context, root)"
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: file path, 2: shell command
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:681
#, php-format
msgid "Root only: save as %1$s (http context: map and limit_*_zone are not allowed in a server block), then nginx -t and a reload. Once per server, shared by every site that uses this plugin. The nginx-light package has no limit_req module: check with %2$s (no output means it is available)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:718
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1267
msgid "Too many filter requests, please retry in a moment."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: concurrent requests, 2: requests per second, 3: burst, 4: requests per minute, 5: burst per IP
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:723
#, php-format
msgid "Server block, only after the http context file is installed (without it nginx -t fails with an unknown zone). Cookie-less filter requests: at most %1$d at once and %2$d per second with a burst of %3$d site-wide, %4$d per minute with a burst of %5$d per IP; beyond that nginx answers 429 with Retry-After. This location takes precedence over page-cache rules in location / for filter URLs."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: log file path
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:733
#, php-format
msgid "ISPConfig: paste it into nginx Directives below the first block. 429 rather than 503, because ISPConfig's Own Error-Documents replace 503 together with its headers. For a calibration phase move limit_req_dry_run on; and limit_conn_dry_run on; out of the trailing comments: nginx then only logs \"limiting requests, dry run\" to %s."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:737
msgid "For a calibration phase remove the # in front of limit_req_dry_run and limit_conn_dry_run: nginx then only logs \"limiting requests, dry run\" to the site's error log."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:743
msgid "Rate limit for filter URLs"
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:753
msgid "robots.txt reaches WordPress through location / - nothing to do. Only if the server block has its own location = /robots.txt (a second one makes nginx -t fail), add try_files $uri /index.php?$args; inside it. A physical robots.txt file in the web root always wins."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:948
msgid "Reject before PHP (VirtualHost)"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: HTTP status code
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:951
#, php-format
msgid "Inside the site's <VirtualHost> (HTTPS and HTTP), then apachectl configtest and a graceful reload. Needs mod_rewrite and mod_headers: apachectl -M | grep -E \"rewrite|headers\". Runs before any .htaccess. The ErrorDocument line sets the body of every %d that Apache itself sends for this host; WordPress answers its own 404 pages through index.php."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:977
msgid "Concurrency cap (mod_qos, server configuration)"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: concurrent requests
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:980
#, php-format
msgid "Global server configuration, outside every <VirtualHost> (root). Needs mod_qos (Debian: libapache2-mod-qos; check apachectl -M | grep qos). At most %d cookie-less filter requests at once, one cap for all sites on this server; any cookie exempts a request, mod_qos answers 429. Search engines share this cap: a User-Agent exemption would let anyone who fakes one bypass it. QS_ClientEventLimitCount is deliberately not used: it bans the whole IP for every URL."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1047
msgid "Rate limits per IP and site-wide (ModSecurity, VirtualHost)"
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: requests, 2: seconds, 3: requests, 4: requests
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1050
#, php-format
msgid "Inside the site's <VirtualHost>. Needs ModSecurity 2 (security2_module) with a SecDataDir for its counters, and mod_headers for Retry-After. Cookie-less filter requests get 429 beyond %1$d per IP in %2$d seconds or %3$d site-wide in 10 seconds; search engines have their own limit of %4$d in 10 seconds. Everything sits in a LocationMatch for the filter URLs: SecRuleEngine On applies there only. If OWASP CRS is loaded without a global SecRuleEngine (Debian's default without modsecurity.conf), CRS runs uninitialised inside this LocationMatch: it blocks nothing there and logs rule 911100 for every filter request. Enable modsecurity.conf globally (for example SecRuleEngine DetectionOnly): CRS then initialises normally, only logs elsewhere and also blocks attacks on the filter URLs - try it on a staging copy first. The site-wide counter is keyed on SERVER_NAME, which follows the request's Host header: in the default VirtualHost or one with a wildcard ServerAlias set UseCanonicalName On, otherwise every Host value gets a fresh site-wide counter. The counters use the RESOURCE and USER collections, because OWASP CRS opens IP and GLOBAL first under its own keys: a rule set of the site's own that opens RESOURCE or USER earlier in the request takes these counters with it. mod_evasive is not recommended: it bans whole IPs and knows no path or cookie."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1063
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1110
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1162
msgid "robots.txt reaches WordPress through WordPress's own .htaccess rules - nothing to do, unless a physical robots.txt file exists in the web root."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1091
msgid "Reject before PHP (.htaccess)"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: HTTP status code
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1094
#, php-format
msgid "Paste it into the .htaccess of the WordPress directory, before the line # BEGIN WordPress - after WordPress's block it never takes effect. Needs AllowOverride FileInfo. The ErrorDocument line sets the body of every %d that Apache itself sends in this directory tree; WordPress answers its own 404 pages through index.php. The plugin never edits a block with these (manual) markers."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1104
msgid "A rate limit cannot be set in .htaccess: mod_qos and ModSecurity are configured at server level. See the tab for the Apache server configuration."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1147
msgid "Reject before PHP (Virtual Host rewrite)"
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1148
msgid "WebAdmin: Virtual Hosts, your host, Rewrite. Set Enable Rewrite to Yes, paste this into Rewrite Rules, save, then Graceful Restart. OpenLiteSpeed cannot set response headers here, so its reject carries no X-Jpkpf-Reject header: the status check cannot recognise it, and the filter script reloads the page on a rejected fragment request. Do not use extraHeaders instead - OpenLiteSpeed ignores its env= condition and would mark every page noindex. .htaccess changes also need a restart on OpenLiteSpeed, which is why the plugin does not manage them there."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1156
msgid "OpenLiteSpeed has no path-based rate limit in its released versions, only the server-wide Per Client Throttling, which applies to every PHP request."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1279
msgid "Inside the site block of the Caddyfile (FrankenPHP: the same block), anywhere before php_fastcgi or php_server, then caddy validate and caddy reload. Rejects and rate limit sit in one route on purpose: a rejected request is answered before rate_limit and never uses up the budget of real visitors. Both parts read the original request URI ({http.request.orig_uri}), so a try_files or rewrite of the site block does not change what they see. If the site wraps php_fastcgi in a handle block, put the route block inside that handle, before php_fastcgi, and keep handle_errors outside it at the site level (Caddy refuses handle_errors inside handle): handle sorts before route and ends the request, so a route outside it never runs."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: requests, 2: seconds, 3: requests, 4: requests, 5: shell command
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1283
#, php-format
msgid "The rate_limit block needs Caddy built with the caddy-ratelimit module (xcaddy build --with github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit; FrankenPHP needs its own build with it). Check with %5$s - without the module Caddy refuses the whole file: then delete the rate_limit and handle_errors blocks, the reject still works. Cookie-less filter requests get 429 with Retry-After beyond %1$d per IP in %2$d seconds or %3$d site-wide in 10 seconds; search engines (by User-Agent) have their own limit of %4$d in 10 seconds. Behind a proxy or CDN use key {client_ip} instead of {remote_host} and the global option servers { trusted_proxies static <ranges> }. handle_errors 429 needs Caddy 2.8 or later and answers every 429 of this site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1294
msgid "Reject before PHP and rate limit (Caddyfile site block)"
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1303
msgid "php_fastcgi (and FrankenPHP's php_server) hands /robots.txt to WordPress - nothing to do, unless the site block has its own respond or file_server rule for /robots.txt, or a physical robots.txt file exists in the web root."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1396
msgid "Ferron 3 is a release candidate: tested with 3.0.0-rc.10. Check it again against 3.0.0 final - the release candidates changed configuration semantics."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1401
msgid "Matchers (ferron.conf, top level)"
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1402
msgid "At the top level of ferron.conf, outside every host block, so every host can use the matchers (3.0.0-rc.10 also accepts them inside the host block). request.uri is the raw request URI, so the reject leaves every percent-encoded path to PHP."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1406
msgid "Inside the site's host block, before the rewrite to /index.php, then ferron validate -c /etc/ferron/ferron.conf and a reload. Needs the matchers block. Rejects answer before PHP with all four headers."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: burst, 2: requests per minute, 3: concurrent requests
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1410
#, php-format
msgid "Rate limit for cookie-less filter requests: per IP a burst of %1$d, then 1 per second (Ferron's smallest rate; the plugin's %2$d per minute cannot be expressed), beyond that 429 with Retry-After. Ferron has no constant rate-limit key, so there is no site-wide rate and search engines cannot get a bucket of their own: they share the per-IP limit. Instead a FastCGI bulkhead lets at most %3$d cookie-less filter requests reach PHP at once; further requests wait for a free connection, they get no 429. Replace <php-backend-url> with the URL of the main fcgi_php (for example tcp://127.0.0.1:9000) and keep the /jpkpf suffix: the bulkhead URL must differ from the main fcgi_php URL, otherwise the limit applies to every PHP request. With a Unix socket the suffix becomes part of the socket path and every cookie-less filter request fails with 500: leave out /jpkpf and spell the same path differently instead, for example unix:///run/php/./php-fpm.sock for unix:///run/php/php-fpm.sock. Both forms rely on Ferron keying the connection pool and its limit by the literal URL string - read in the 3.0.0-rc.10 source, not documented, so check it again against 3.0.0 final; a second PHP-FPM listen socket or port (its own pool) is the robust alternative. Ferron answers the 429 with its default error page and no Cache-Control header; a 429 is not cached by default (RFC 9111), so nothing needs adding. Behind a proxy or CDN set client_ip_from_header with trusted_proxy, otherwise the per-IP limit counts the proxy."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1419
msgid "Reject before PHP and rate limit (host block)"
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1428
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1500
msgid "The front-controller rewrite to /index.php hands /robots.txt to WordPress - nothing to do, unless a physical robots.txt file exists in the web root."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1462
msgid "Reject before PHP"
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1, 2, 3: literal URL escapes (%2B, %2F, %70age), not placeholders to translate
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1465
#, php-format
msgid "No reject rule for Ferron 2.8 in this version: none has been tested. Ferron 2 decodes percent escapes such as %1$s, %2$s or %3$s and merges slashes before any rule runs, and PHP receives that same decoded URI, so WordPress already counts what a server rule would see and keeps rejecting over-cap URLs itself. Ferron 3 can reject before PHP on the raw request.uri."
msgstr ""

#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1486
msgid "Rate limit for filter URLs (ferron.kdl host block)"
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: burst, 2: requests per minute
#: includes/server-rules-render.php:1489
#, php-format
msgid "Inside the site's host block in ferron.kdl, before the rewrite and fcgi_php, then restart Ferron. Cookie-less filter requests: per IP a burst of %1$d, then 1 per second (the plugin's %2$d per minute cannot be expressed), beyond that 429. A missing header is \"\" in Ferron 2 conditions, so is_equal \"{header:Cookie}\" \"\" matches exactly the requests without any cookie. Ferron 2 has no site-wide cap and no bucket of their own for search engines: they share the per-IP limit. The 429 is Ferron's default error page without Retry-After or Cache-Control; a 429 is not cached by default (RFC 9111). Behind a proxy or CDN the per-IP limit counts the proxy."
msgstr ""

